- **Stable Vocabulary & Contracts:** Consistent use of terms like “view”, “manager”, and “engine” means you avoid confusion and accidental overlap.

### What Could Cause It To Backfire/Bloat

- **If Controlled Migration Is Ignored:** If you—or the AI tooling—try to clean/reshape everything at once, there’s a risk of breaking features, losing history, and just shuffling confusion (“big-bang” refactors always risk new hidden coupling and junk).
- **If Feature Boundaries Are Poorly Defined:** If you’re not careful about what “feature” means, you might re-bundle old unrelated files into new “feature” folders that are just as hard to trace.
- **If Vocabulary/Conventions Drift:** If AI mixes up the canonical definitions (e.g. starts calling components "managers" or vice versa), or modifies section/item names in routes, then the result will be more confusion.
- **Neglecting End-to-End Verification:** If you don’t test each feature’s flow after it’s moved/cleaned, you might introduce silent breakage or regression that's hard to trace.

### Ways to Mitigate New Bloat/Complexity

- Always stop and trace ownership, dependency, and trigger-flow before refactoring any one feature.
- Never move files just to flatten the structure—do it because the result is clearer, not just different.
- Review every directory and import after migration to make sure you haven’t created new “weak” groupings.
