- Feature folders start collecting files that aren’t clearly related (smells like a new “junk drawer”).
- The “shared” or “utils” folders start growing again for vague reasons.
- You’re unsure what would break if a single file is changed/removed.
- Section/item IDs or routing logic are changed without explicit, reviewed reason.
- Legacy and SPA code intermingle, or vendor code leaks outside “external” or “assets”.

### Conclusion

When diligently followed, this approach is much more likely to reduce actual bloat, make the codebase more understandable/manageable, and set the stage for safe, incremental feature work. If the rules are ignored—especially around controlled migration, “feature” definition, and vocabulary discipline—it could just shuffle messiness or even add confusion. Early, continuous review and incremental, purposeful moves are key.

If you see new “mega folders”, ambiguous imports, or weakly named/organized new code cropping up, stop and revisit the core rules. If instead, every feature can truly be explained, traced, and tested end-to-end with minimal repo jumping, you’re on the right path. 

